# Job Radar - Core Architecture

Canonical source: `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root. Keep this file in sync with it.

Stack: Python 3.12 + Flask 3 + Jinja + vanilla JS/CSS (no build step) + SQLite + Anthropic SDK.

## Structure

```text
run.py -> jobradar/cli/main.py
jobradar/
  app.py  container.py          app factory; composition root (one Container per request / task)
  cli/                          argparse + report/stats/dry-run commands
  domain/<feature>/             jobs, applications, prefilter, scoring, alerts, cv, preferences, settings,
                                usage, home, report
    <feature>.py                entity (dataclass + behaviour)
    <feature>_repository.py     SQL for its own table(s)
    <feature>_queries.py        reusable SQL fragments
    <feature>_service.py        use cases
    <feature>_mapper.py         entity -> view model / JSON
    <feature>_view.py           view-model dataclasses (DTOs)
    <feature>_routes.py         Flask Blueprint (thin)
  sources/                      one <name>_source.py per feed + source_registry.py + feed_collector.py
  pipeline/                     pipeline_steps, task_runner, scheduler, task_routes (orchestration only)
  lib/                          database, llm_client, mail_client, email_text, http_client, env_file, key_value_store
  shared/utils  shared/constants
  web/                          request_scope, responses, security, template_helpers, icons
  templates/components/{atoms,molecules,organisms}  templates/layouts  templates/pages
  static/css  static/js/{lib,components,pages}
tests/                          pytest, mirrors jobradar/
```

- Group by feature, not technical type. Name files `<feature>_<role>.py`.
- Keep files under ~200 lines. Split by responsibility.

## Layers (dependencies point inward only)

Route/CLI -> Service -> Repository -> lib/database. Services use Entities for rules, Mappers for output, and lib clients for I/O.

- Routes: parse the request, call one service method, render or `reply()`. No SQL, rules, LLM calls or `json.loads`.
- Services: no `flask` imports. Dependencies are injected via `__init__`.
- Entities: state and rules, no I/O.
- Repositories: parameterised SQL only (`?`), no business rules.
- `lib/` and `shared/` never import from `domain/`.

## SOLID

- SRP: one reason to change per module/class.
- OCP: a new feed or status means adding a file or constant, not an `elif` chain.
- LSP: every `JobSource` returns `list[JobPosting]` and raises the same error type.
- ISP: small Protocols (`JobSource`, `CompanyBoardSource`; clients injected via `container.py`).
- DIP: inject dependencies. `container.py` wires them, and tests pass fakes.

## Keeping the structure

Every change lands in its feature folder. Refactor-only commits must not change behaviour.
Split a file by responsibility before it passes ~200 lines. Only `container.py` constructs services.

## Product invariants (never break)

1. A job is scored at most once. Duplicates never reach the LLM.
2. The prefilter makes no LLM or network calls.
3. Every Claude call goes through `lib/llm_client` and logs usage with a `purpose`.
4. Each email goes to Claude at most once (`emails_seen`).
5. Automatic status changes only move forward.
6. Nothing is sent or submitted without a user click. No auto-apply.
7. Secrets stay in `.env`: never logged, sent to Claude or committed.
8. The server binds to 127.0.0.1. Keep the same-origin POST check.
9. Schema changes are additive only.

Reuse before creating new: search `shared/`, `components/` and `static/js/lib` first.
If a request conflicts with these rules, ask before writing code.
